pthreads: a task pthreads did not make gets a record of its own, freed when it ends

0008 made a task pthreads did not make (an SDL thread, which SDL2.module
starts with CreateNewProc and NP_Entry; a Workbench helper; any
CreateNewProc'd process) the main thread, so it shared the main thread's
thread-local values: GCC's emutls gave every such task the main thread's
__thread block, and Allegro's per-thread state in Kraptor's SDL_allegro thread
would be the main thread's. Dale chose (2 Nov 2026, 18:55) "own record +
cleanup": such a task gets a pthreads record and TLS block of its own, and the
record is freed when the task ends.

Registration. The first time such a task calls pthread_self() (directly, or
through pthread_getspecific/setspecific and so emutls), it is given a free slot
of the thread table, under thread_sem, the lock pthread_create uses; the table
grows as for pthread_create (FreeSlot, shared with it). Its ThreadInfo is
marked foreign: started, detached (pthread_join and pthread_detach answer
EINVAL), no parent (pthread_cancel answers ESRCH), its own tlsvalues, cleanup
list and cancel state; pthread_exit in it runs the cleanup handlers and
returns, as in the main thread, instead of longjmp-ing to a StarterFunc it
never had.

Cleanup. OS 3 has no exec call-back when a task ends, so each foreign record
is tied to its task with a token: a one-entry MemList (12 bytes: a magic
number, the slot and this program's table address) added to the task's own
tc_MemEntry list, which RemTask frees with the task. A sweep finds the records
whose task has gone: under Disable() it walks SysBase->TaskReady and TaskWait
(and ThisTask) and each task's tc_MemEntry (at most 64 nodes, skipping a list
that was never initialised), marking the records whose token it finds on the
task they name. Every foreign record not marked is taken out of use; then,
with thread_sem released, its key destructors run (PTHREAD_DESTRUCTOR_ITERATIONS
passes, as StarterFunc runs them for a thread that ends, but in the sweeping
task), its leftover cleanup handlers are freed, and its slot is free again.
The sweep runs when a foreign task registers, at pthread_create and at
pthread_key_create, and only when foreign records exist. The token also stops
a new task that exec happens to allocate where an ended one was from taking
that task's record: pthread_self() takes a foreign record only if its token is
on the calling task's own list.

Table full. If no slot is left after the sweep (PTHREAD_THREADS_MAX, 2019,
the main thread included), or memory is short, the task is the main thread
(id 0), as with 0008, and never a NULL record. A task refused for a full table
gets a token for no slot, so it stays the main thread for its whole life (its
TLS block never changes under it) and later calls answer at once. If the token
itself can't be allocated it is the main thread and asks again next time.

Exit. __pthread_Exit_Func no longer waits for foreign records (they are not
joinable): it takes this program's tokens off the tasks still running and
drops the records, without destructors, as exit() does for the main thread's
values.

The NULL checks of 0008 in pthread_getspecific/setspecific stay.

tests/pthreads/foreigntls.c: three NP_Entry children alive at once each get
their own id, __thread block and key value, with nothing leaking between them
or into the main thread; their ids are not joinable or cancellable; after they
end, a pthread_key_create runs the key's destructor for each; ten more rounds
reuse the same three records with every destructor run (33 of 33); a
pthread_create'd thread is unaffected. "foreigntls full" fills the table with
2018 parked children: the 2019th is the main thread, stays so after the table
has emptied, and children started then get records again.

